THE VSS QUESTION -- THE HAND-OFF'S PREDICTED SURPRISE, MEASURED
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  The hand-off flagged that four VSS bulk landings (XN0-XN3) were released onto their tie
  rings and one VSS label moved to (229.647, 444.033), calling it "safe by construction but
  not a measurement". Here is the measurement.
  Extracted netlist of the SHORTENED layout vs extracted netlist of the PRE-SHORTENING
  layout (both through the same coil view) differ by EXACTLY ONE LINE:
      < .subckt routed VDD Vcont voutn voutp VSS Iref
      > .subckt routed VDD Vcont voutn voutp Iref VSS
  That is top-cell PORT ORDER only -- same six ports, same set. Every device instance line,
  every subckt, every net name is byte-identical. Port order follows label position, so the
  relocated VSS label fully explains it and it carries no connectivity meaning (netgen
  matches by name; "Cell pin lists are equivalent").
  All four bulk terminals land on VSS in the extraction (XN0/XN1/XN2 4th terminal, XN3 3rd).
  CONCLUSION: the tie-ring release changed ZERO connectivity. Claim confirmed by measurement.

